write('R2_INSTRUCTION_COMPLIANCE.md','''# R2 six-rule compliance and ablation audit

The exact frozen experiments/r12-01/R2.md bytes remain unchanged and are hash-bound in the input manifest. The scored BASELINE already supplies a call-count plan, an eight-minute allowance, targeted verification, a git-diff/submit finish and a dangerous unconditional best-guess-edit clause. All six R2 rules were added as one treatment. One run per task/candidate cannot isolate a causal effect of any one rule.

| Frozen R2 instruction | Observed adherence | Observed action difference / information | Benefit | Cost / conflict | Disposition |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1. Smallest Issue-supported production edit early | Minimal final patches; first edits at 256/326/134s, pytest later than BASELINE | R2 edits all tasks; BASELINE only pytest, R1 no xarray edit | Delivery coverage associated with R2; rule-specific cause UNKNOWN | Too-late inspection still occurs; inherited blind-edit deadline can conflict with Issue-supported evidence | Retain evidence requirement; Candidate A tests one inherited decision rule instead of adding a compulsory earlier deadline |
| 2. get_status after localization/edit and before long work; avoid redundant queries | Zero calls across R2 | Static max time/calls/turns appears in the initial prompt; dynamic call-budget warnings appear 3/11/2 times. No dynamic time query/date command observed | Both sources can support call allocation; actual remaining-time adaptation UNKNOWN | A free tool still costs an additional inference round. Avoid-redundant wording can be read too broadly when only calls were reported | Do not label useless merely from zero calls; time-vs-call information distinction needs a separate future ablation, not a third candidate here |
| 3. Budget from remaining time/calls and reserve check/diff/submit | Submits preserve about 58/47/216s at call time; xarray consumes all 28 charged calls | Automatic call warnings are real alternative budget information, but give no seconds | Successful delivery observed, no causal attribution | Mostly duplicates inherited call plan/finalization; cannot prove time-driven adaptation | Preserve local reserve intent; no global-clock claim |
| 4. Stop broad optional exploration near deadline | Mixed: Django reverses its fix late; xarray continues probe design; pytest searches testing coverage after smoke | Some compatibility/negative lookups add new information; some unchanged subsets do not add bytes | No experimentally identified individual-rule gain | Must distinguish broad exploration from required regression work | Consider evidence-threshold or closure ablation; never blanket-ban source reads |
| 5. Bound verification by time after finalization reserve | No observed explicit dynamic timeout calculation | Official run_command already clamps execution to local remaining time with a documented floor | Host guard protects a command ceiling; it does not reserve future validation/submission | Rule partly overlaps host semantics; omitting a test on an assumed reserve risks correctness | Preserve necessary tests; do not claim this instruction was enforced by the model |
| 6. Inspect diff and submit while time/calls remain | All three submit nonempty valid final patches; zero explicit git diff | edit_file returns the actual local hunk to the model. submit_patch internally collects/filters full diff and reports size/files. Actual cognitive review UNKNOWN | Scope-clean final patches observed; not proof of full diff review or rule causality | Overlaps inherited finish rule; R1 premature mixed-scope submit shows why scope inspection still matters | Candidate B may test one version-bound closure replacement; no automatic removal of diff/control checks |

## Why no get_status?

The trajectory demonstrates non-use, not the model's reason. Initial system/user messages expose the static limits. The actual SDK low-budget warning attaches remaining **call counts**, not time; R2 receives them in all three sessions. No R2 run_command obtains the current time or a reliable global/session elapsed clock. Implicit time estimation, prompt ambiguity and sampling are plausible explanations, not established causes. The three R2 sessions still submit with time left, so a measured repair loss caused specifically by not querying status is not demonstrated. Lack of dynamic time evidence is a risk, not a fabricated observed failure.

The real official get_status was CPU-exercised on an eight-minute/28-call context: it returns local remaining seconds/calls and patch metadata, not global seconds or future task count. It does not debit a charged call. Model inference after calling it can still consume time. A rule that required frequent redundant status calls could make the Agent slower; a rule that never checks time can also fail on long operations. No ablation resolves that tradeoff yet.

## Why no explicit git diff?

Every R2 production edit returns a diff in edit_file's actual tool response. The last edit's diff therefore exposes the final small hunk. Full-patch internal collection occurs inside submit_patch; the CPU contract confirms git add -N and git diff --binary. Its response displays only size/file count, not full diff text or a correctness judgment. Reading that hunk, reading changed source, and cognitively reviewing it are not the same event; cognitive review remains UNKNOWN.

R2's final patches are clean by independent review; no observed R2 loss is attributable to the missing explicit command. However, local edit hunks cannot reveal unrelated untracked diagnostics. R1 Django first submits two files and later correctly removes its script/resubmits one file. BASELINE xarray receives status ok for zero bytes twice. This is evidence for a production-scope/nonempty check, not evidence to require redundant diff commands after an unchanged validated patch.

## Conflicts and what an ablation could establish

The inherited instruction to edit by a numbered call even when unsure conflicts with evidence-supported repair and with allowing enough time for necessary diagnostics. Baseline's at-most-two verification calls also conflicts with the need to correct malformed verification or revalidate a changed production version. These conflicts were present during all arms; do not silently repair the frozen control.

Candidate A proposes changing one inherited edit-decision bullet to an evidence threshold. Candidate B proposes changing only R2 rule 6 to version-bound closure. They are alternatives and are not combined. Rules 2/3/5 partly duplicate supplied limits and host guards; their marginal benefit, cost and whether they merely add prompt tokens are UNKNOWN. A future preregistered ablation is required to answer that question. No instruction is declared causally effective or useless solely from these three outcomes.
''')

